Description: Distro Custom: build your own Debian or Devuan distribution
 D!sco (Distro Custom) builds your own distribution from Debian (stable,
 testing, sid) or Devuan, with Debian's own tools: debootstrap or mmdebstrap,
 chroot, squashfs, xorriso and, if you like, live-build. No Ubuntu parts
 (no casper, no PPAs) are used.
 .
 It builds for PCs (64-bit and 32-bit), 64-bit ARM computers with UEFI,
 the Raspberry Pi (1, Zero, 2, 3, 4, 400 and 5) and RISC-V. Bases for other
 processors are made on this computer through qemu-user. PCs and UEFI
 machines get a hybrid live ISO (BIOS and UEFI); boards get an SD-card image
 laid out like Debian's own Raspberry Pi images.
 .
 The Builder page holds the whole distribution: a preset, the name, the
 platform, Debian or Devuan release, desktop (GNOME, KDE Plasma, Xfce,
 Cinnamon, MATE, LXQt, LXDE, Budgie, Enlightenment, Eduka-Desktop, i3, Sway,
 labwc and more), init system (systemd, SysVinit, OpenRC, runit), installer
 (Calamares, none or your own packages), package lists with tick boxes and
 an application catalog, and the steps: Prepare, Build base, Install
 packages, Overlay & scripts, Make image, or Build all.
 .
 Every part can be refined: repositories, identity and the files of
 /etc (os-release, lsb-release, issue, hostname, motd), users, language,
 kernel, boot loaders, boot menu, Plymouth, wallpaper and login screen,
 themes, sounds, a welcome screen, the bash prompt, browser policies, a PDF
 about your system on every desktop, and the Calamares installer. Checks
 before building stop what would break the image, and it can be tested in
 QEMU.
 .
 D!sco was called DistroForge (0.9 Beta) and Eduka-Customizer (up to 0.17
 Alpha); the old commands keep working.
